---
import SiteLayout from '../layouts/SiteLayout.astro';
import hero from '../assets/shilshole.webp';
import smallHero from '../assets/shilshole-small.webp';
---
<SiteLayout
  title="Open chart data for marine apps"
  description="Add inspectable NOAA chart features to your marine web app. Explore Puget Sound or use the open PMTiles archive with MapLibre."
>
    <section class="hero" aria-labelledby="hero-title">
      <div class="hero-copy">
        <p class="label"><span class="status-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span> Puget Sound preview</p>
        <h1 id="hero-title">Open chart data for marine apps.</h1>
        <p class="lede">Add inspectable NOAA chart features to your map with a single PMTiles archive. Load it in MapLibre without running your own chart conversion pipeline or tile server.</p>
        <div class="actions">
          <a class="action primary" href="/inspect.html">Open the inspection map <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a>
          <a class="action secondary" href="/integrate/">Use the data</a>
        </div>
        <p class="notice">Experimental chart data. Not for navigation.</p>
      </div>
      <figure class="chart">
        <img src={hero.src}
             srcset={`${smallHero.src} 420w, ${hero.src} 755w`}
             sizes="(min-width: 70rem) 32rem, (min-width: 55rem) 46vw, 94vw"
             width="755" height="610" fetchpriority="high"
             alt="CharTiles map centered on the two Shilshole Bay entrance buoys, with nearby depth soundings and contours.">
        <figcaption>
          <span>Shilshole Bay, Washington</span>
          <span>NOAA ENC · CharTiles preview</span>
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
    </section>

    <section class="benefits" aria-labelledby="benefits-title">
      <h2 id="benefits-title">What you can do with CharTiles</h2>
      <div class="benefit-grid">
        <article class="card">
          <h3>Put chart features in your app</h3>
          <p>Use the public archive to put buoys, lights, wrecks, and soundings on a MapLibre map. Serve the same file from your own storage when you need to.</p>
          <a href="/integrate/">See how to integrate <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a>
        </article>
        <article class="card">
          <h3>See what the chart recorded</h3>
          <p>Select a feature and read its recorded attributes, including depth and sounding quality where present. Trace it back to the NOAA source cell.</p>
          <a href="/inspect.html?view=features#18/47.6766/-122.4145">Explore near Shilshole <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a>
        </article>
        <article class="card">
          <h3>Review charts offline</h3>
          <p>Build a local bundle with the viewer and Puget Sound archive. Once set up, you can inspect features with the connection turned off.</p>
          <a href="https://github.com/ivanoats/chartiles/blob/main/docs/offline-bundle.md">Build an offline bundle <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a>
        </article>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="developers" class="developer-panel" aria-labelledby="developers-title">
      <div>
        <p class="label">For developers</p>
        <h2 id="developers-title">Start with one regional archive.</h2>
        <p>Read the release manifest for the archive URL, build date, and NOAA cell revisions. Then use the PMTiles protocol to add the vector source in MapLibre.</p>
        <div class="actions">
          <a class="action primary" href="https://chartiles-data.ivanstorck.workers.dev/charts/manifest.json">Open the release manifest <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a>
          <a class="action secondary" href="/integrate/">Read the integration guide <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a>
        </div>
      </div>
      <ol class="integration-steps">
        <li><strong>Find the archive.</strong> Resolve <code>pmtiles_url</code> against the data service root.</li>
        <li><strong>Add it to MapLibre.</strong> Register the PMTiles protocol and add a vector source.</li>
        <li><strong>Inspect a feature.</strong> Draw a layer such as <code>WRECKS</code> and use its <code>source_cell</code> to look up the NOAA revision.</li>
      </ol>
    </section>
</SiteLayout>
